  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA shortwave and associated cold front will move across the area late \ntoday into Tuesday. Showers and thunderstorms are expected to form \nalong and ahead of the front, with scattered severe thunderstorms \npossible during the late afternoon, evening, and overnight. Heading \ninto Tuesday, the lingering frontal boundary could still be situated \nin northwest Minnesota, where there is a chance for isolated severe \nthunderstorms, primarily east of the Red River Valley. In the post \nfrontal environment, cooler highs in the 70s to low 80s are expected \nTuesday through Thursday, with warmer temperatures this weekend as \nweak ridging builds across the western CONUS and High Plains. \nAdditional shortwaves bring showers and thunderstorms back into the \nforecast for Saturday and Sunday.\n\n...Scattered Severe Storms This Evening and Overnight...\n\nInstability is slowly building this afternoon as warmer temps and \nhigher moisture levels work into the area. Dew points have increased \ninto the lower to middle 60s in most areas, with SBCAPE values \ngenerally in the 1500 to 2000 J/Kg range early this afternoon. Model \nsoundings show the potential for 2500 to 3000 J/Kg by early evening. \nMid level shear is present, but unidirectional above 700 mb, leading \nto straight hodographs and a continued support for splitting \nsupercells. It is worth noting, however, that given the tendency to \nsplit, these supercells are also likely to interact and eventually \nform clusters. The primary hazard during the afternoon and early \nevening will be large hail to the size of golf balls, with a low \nchance for an isolated tornado. Due to relatively weak low level \nshear, tornado risk will be dependent on longer lived supercells \nthat avoid interaction with nearby cells. With DCAPE in the 1100 to \n1400 J/Kg range, damaging wind gusts to 70 mph are supported \nlate this afternoon through mid evening.",
